CORE
main.cpp
--cpp11
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ char and signed char: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ int and wchar_t: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ long and long long: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.4\] line \d+ unsigned short and char16_t: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.5\] line \d+ member of the primary: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.6\] line \d+ __is_same: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.7\] line \d+ promotion: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.8\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
N5008 [basic.fundamental]/1, /8-9, [temp.type]/1: wchar_t and int, char
and signed char, long and long long, char16_t and unsigned short are
distinct types with the same representation.  The front end represents
each pair by one bit-vector type that differs only in its #c_type
comment, which irept::operator== ignores: kind<int> used the explicit
specialization kind<wchar_t>, kind<char> used kind<signed char>, and
__is_same(int, wchar_t) was true (bin53).  In libstdc++'s <format> this
excluded int from the integer formatter (`!__is_one_of<_Tp, char,
wchar_t, char16_t, char32_t>'), so formatter<int, char> had no parse().
Explicit-specialization matching and __is_same now compare the type
names, and [conv.prom]/2 promotes wchar_t and char32_t to int and
unsigned int although the representation does not change (libc++'s
__get_property passes `(__code_point << 11) | 0x7ffu' to
ranges::upper_bound over uint32_t).  char8_t is still unsigned char
(c_preprocess.cpp undefines __cpp_char8_t).  g++ 13 and clang++ 18 accept
this program, and it fails only its WRONG assertion.
